{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":29,"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_586753098#16_1198893323"},"record":{"query":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_586753098#16_1198893323","op":"document"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_586753098#16_1198893323","document":"Jaime Escalante | Encyclopedia.com ... Escalante, Jaime: 1930—: Educator Escalante, Jaime: 1930—: Educator Jaime Escalante: 1930 —: Educator Early Teaching Experience At a Glance . . . Immigrated to the United States Challenged Students to Excel Became a National Hero Joined English for Children Initiative Sources Books Periodicals On-line Jaime Escalante Jaime Escalante Public Acclaim Program Continues Further Reading  ... The student body at Garfield was 95% Latino and 80% poor. When Escalante arrived, he quickly learned that there were no computers and that he would be teaching math instead. That was not his only disappointment at Garfield. The students were unruly, poorly educated, disrespectful, and sometimes violent, which was not what Escalante was accustomed to. \\\" They were using their fingers adding stuff at the board,\\\" Escalante told People Weekly in April of 1988. \\\" They came in without supplies, with nothing. Total chaos.\\\" Escalante believed that his first year at Garfield would be his last. Escalante returned to Garfield for a second year partly due to his passion for teaching and partly because of his commitment to his family. He knew that his sons had more opportunities in the United States than they had in Bolivia, and he was trying to make the best of his situation.","ok":true,"op":"document"},"time":13},"model_calls":29,"document_judgments":20,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
