{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":58,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2170"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2170"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1039848851#14_1878661556","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2170","rank":3,"snippet":"didn't dampen the enthusiasm of many Garfield graduates, who came from all over Los Angeles and beyond to show their support for their former teacher, Jaime Escalante. Escalante's former students recently learned he is in the end stages of bladder cancer that has spread throughout his body. The medical costs have depleted Escalante's savings, and the students are determined to help out. 'Barrio Kids' To the astonishment of the outside world, Escalante taught many of these returning graduates math — advanced math, like trigonometry and calculus. Garfield educates some of Los Angeles' poorest students, many of them from immigrant families, and many of whom never conceived of <cover>college as a possibility. But Escalante did. Web Resources YouTube: Jaime Escalante On Being A Teacher YouTube: Actor Edward James Olmos As Jaime Escalante in \"Stand And Deliver\" The Bolivian-born teacher believed math was the portal to any success his students could achieve </cover>later in life.\" , \"start_char\": 6046.000000 , \"title\": \"Teacher Portrayed in 'Stand And Deliver' Dies : NPR\" , \"url\": \"https://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=125380015\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1039848851#14_1878661556","rank":3,"relevance":1}]},"time":17},"model_calls":58,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":58,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2170"},"model_calls":58,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":58,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2170"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2170"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_586753098#19_1198898316","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2170","rank":4,"snippet":"He was not afraid to yell at students for being late or lazy, but he also gave them encouragement and taught them to believe in themselves. He told them that they <cover>could succeed at everything if they had the ganas, the desire. He decorated his classroom with inspirational sayings and sports posters. He was imaginative in his techniques to get his points across. He once brought a meat cleaver and an apple to class to teach his students fractions. He would wear funny hats, make jokes about sex, and do whatever he could think of to get his students' attention — and he was successful. He soon gained the students' respect and they nicknamed him Kemo Sabe, the man who knows, which was Tonto's name for the famous Lone Ranger. Garfield's students began to rise to the challenge set forth by Escalante. When he introduced calculus in 1979 he had five students in the class.\" , \"start_char\": 8521.000000 , \"title\": \"Jaime Escalante | </cover>Encyclopedia.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/social-sciences-and-law/political-science-biographies/jaime-escalante\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1039848851#14_1878661556","rank":3,"relevance":1}]},"time":9},"model_calls":58,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
