{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":75,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"jaime escalante\" \"ganas\" \"desire\" students (+ college future success))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"jaime escalante\" \"ganas\" \"desire\" students (+ college future success))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1039848851#15_1878663180","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2185","rank":1,"snippet":"\"Garfield educates some of Los Angeles' poorest students, many of them from immigrant families, and many of whom never conceived of college as a possibility. But Escalante did. Web Resources YouTube: Jaime Escalante On Being A Teacher YouTube: Actor Edward James Olmos As <cover>Jaime Escalante in \"Stand And Deliver\" The Bolivian-born teacher believed math was the portal to any success his students could achieve later in life. So before school formally began, and after school ended, his door was open for extra help. And the students came on weekends and worked through holidays to prepare for the hardest exam of all — the Advanced Placement calculus exam. \"It was hard,\" says Mark Baca, who now works with a Los Angeles nonprofit. \" But he changed the minds of people all over the world about barrio kids.\" Escalante's barrio kids became stars, exemplars of what can happen when knowledge-thirsty kids with ganas — a deep desire — </cover>to succeed combine with a dedicated teacher with ganas for their success.\" , \"start_char\": 6714.000000 , \"title\": \"Teacher Portrayed in 'Stand And Deliver' Dies : NPR\" , \"url\": \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1039848851#15_1878663180","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":75},"model_calls":75,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":75,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2185"},"model_calls":75,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":75,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2185"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2185"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_838402051#2_1023861486","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2185","rank":2,"snippet":"Cast Historical accuracy Reception Accolades Legacy See also References External links \" , \"segment\": \"Escalante instructs his class under the philosophy of <cover>ganas, roughly translating to \"desire\" or \"motivation\". The students begin taking summer classes in advanced mathematics with Escalante, who must withstand the cynicism of the other faculty who feel that the students are not capable of this. As they struggle with the lower expectations that they face in society, Escalante works hard to teach and encourage them, and they pass the AP Calculus exam. To the dismay of both Escalante and the students, the Educational Testing Service questions the success of the students, insisting there is too much overlap in their errors and suggesting the students cheated. Escalante defends his students and feels that the allegations are based more on racial and economic perceptions. He offers to have the students retake the test months later, and the students all succeed in passing the test, despite having only a day to prepare. Cast Edward James Olmos as Jaime Escalante</cover>Estelle Harris as Secretary Virginia Paris as Raquel Ortega Will Gotay as Francisco \"Pancho\" Garcia Ingrid Oliu as Guadalupe \"Lupe\" Escobar Carmen Argenziano as Molina Rosanna ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_838402051#2_1023861486","rank":2,"relevance":2}]},"time":11},"model_calls":75,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
