{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":77,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"jaime escalante\" students (+ \"college\" \"careers\" \"jobs\" \"future\") (+ \"math\" \"calculus\" \"education\") (+ \"encouraged\" \"inspired\" \"motivated\" \"persuaded\"))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"jaime escalante\" students (+ \"college\" \"careers\" \"jobs\" \"future\") (+ \"math\" \"calculus\" \"education\") (+ \"encouraged\" \"inspired\" \"motivated\" \"persuaded\"))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_345449932#0_824515965","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2187","rank":1,"snippet":"transformative power of an effective teacher is something almost all of us have experienced and understand on a personal level. If we were particularly fortunate, we had numerous exceptional teachers who made school an exciting and interesting place. Those teachers possessed a passion for the subjects that they taught and genuine care for the students with whom they worked. They inspired us to play with ideas, think deeply about the subject matter, take on more challenging work, and even pursue <cover>careers in a particular field of study. Some exceptional teachers achieve celebrity status, such as Jaime Escalante, the math teacher who inspired the film Stand and Deliver, but thousands of unsung heroes go unrecognized in their remarkable work with students </cover>on a daily basis. Qualities of Effective Teachers We know intuitively that these highly effective teachers can have an enriching effect on the daily lives of children and their lifelong educational and career aspirations.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"The Power of an Effective Teacher and Why We Should Assess It\" , \"url\": \"http://www.ascd.org/publications/books/104136/chapters/The-Power-","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_345449932#0_824515965","rank":1,"relevance":1}]},"time":86},"model_calls":77,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":77,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"model_calls":77,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":77,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_345449932#1_824518445","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2187","rank":2,"snippet":"Effective Promoting Teacher Effectiveness Assessing Teacher Effectiveness Concerns Possibilities Conclusion Endnotes Requesting Permission \" , \"segment\": \"If we were particularly fortunate, we had numerous exceptional teachers who made school an exciting and interesting place. Those teachers possessed a passion for the subjects that they taught and genuine care for the students with whom they worked. They inspired us to play with ideas, think deeply about the subject matter, take on more challenging work, and even pursue <cover>careers in a particular field of study. Some exceptional teachers achieve celebrity status, such as Jaime Escalante, the math teacher who inspired the film Stand and Deliver, but thousands of unsung heroes go unrecognized in their remarkable work with students </cover>on a daily basis. Qualities of Effective Teachers We know intuitively that these highly effective teachers can have an enriching effect on the daily lives of children and their lifelong educational and career aspirations. We now know empirically that these effective teachers also have a direct influence in enhancing student learning. Years of research on teacher quality support the fact that effective teachers not only make students feel good about school and learning, but also that their work actually results ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_345449932#1_824518445","rank":2,"relevance":1}]},"time":6},"model_calls":77,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":77,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"model_calls":77,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":77,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_638824153#6_1349660670","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2187","rank":3,"snippet":"life \" , \"segment\": \"Just a couple of year later in 1982 eighteen of Escalante’s students passed the Advanced Placement Calculus exam. The results seemed faked, and therefore several students had to re-do the review, and twelve of the students who agreed to do the exam again, passed it. The number of students successfully passing the exam kept doubling from year to year, and Escalante got nationwide attention. In 1983 Jaime Escalante was invited to teach a <cover>Calculus class at East Los Angeles College. Recognition The success of Jaime Escalante was recognized and was real. His methods worked, and the number of students passing the exam was increasing which inspired </cover>a book and a movie to be produced. The book written by Jay Mathews was released in 1988 and was called “Escalante: The Best Teacher in America,” and a movie “Stand and Deliver” followed shortly. Further work After Escalante’s work became publicly known, he continued working and preparing students for the calculus exam. Some people disliked his popularity, and Jaime Escalante began receiving threat mail.\" , \"start_char\": 3047.000000 , \"title\": \"Jaime ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_638824153#6_1349660670","rank":3,"relevance":1}]},"time":7},"model_calls":77,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":77,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"model_calls":77,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":77,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2187"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_638824153#4_1349656986","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2187","rank":4,"snippet":"\" , \"segment\": \"Precisely at the same time, the accreditation of the school was questioned, but Jame Escalante did not agree to lower the standards for his students to save the school. He did precisely the opposite – he raised the rules, which forced students to study more and with a higher sense of responsibility. He offered his <cover>students a perspective of good jobs and careers if they paid enough attention to his subject – maths. The perspective fields he provided were electronics, computers, engineering and some others. Jaime Escalante's initiative in the school was not welcomed too openly, until the change of the principal in the school. As soon as Henry Gradillas started working as the principal, he motivated Escalante to continue what he had already begun. Henry Gradillas reduced the number of primary math </cover>classes and increased the number of algebra classes, and enforced some other ideas of Escalante to achieve better results. Calculus class In 1978 Jaime Escalante first started teaching Calculus. Initially, only five students attended it, and only two passed, but the following years the ratio increased exponentially. In 1979 nine students took the class, and seven passed the test, and the ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_638824153#4_1349656986","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":4},"model_calls":77,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
