{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35620","model_call":81,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2145"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2145"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_586753098#3_1198872844","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2145","rank":6,"snippet":": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_586753098#3_1198872844\" , \"end_char\": 2588.000000 , \"headings\": \"Escalante, Jaime: 1930—: Educator Escalante, Jaime: 1930—: Educator Jaime Escalante: 1930 —: Educator Early Teaching Experience At a Glance . . . Immigrated to the United States Challenged Students to Excel Became a National Hero Joined English for Children Initiative Sources Books Periodicals On-line Jaime Escalante<cover>Jaime Escalante Public Acclaim Program Continues Further Reading \" , \"segment\": \"Escalante's father was an abusive alcoholic, so his mother took the children and moved to La Paz. When Escalante was 14 years old his mother sent him to San Calixto, a prestigious Jesuit high school, where his favorite subjects were math and engineering. </cover>When Escalante was a teenager his father died, which meant that the family would not be able to afford to send him to engineering school as he had hoped. Instead, Escalante did odd jobs until he was 19 years old, when he briefly joined the army to fight against leftist rebellions. When his service was over, a friend convinced Escalante to go to college at Normal Superior in order to become ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_638824153#3_1349655263","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_586753098#15_1198891641","rank":4,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_2119863051#4_2281921572","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":81,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
